## Project Summary: Automated In Silico Screening of HIV-1 Protease Inhibitors
Abstract
This project demonstrates the execution of a fully automated, high-throughput computational drug discovery pipeline to identify potential inhibitors for the HIV-1 Protease enzyme (PDB: 1HSG). By integrating cheminformatics, molecular docking, and physics-based simulations into a seamless, code-driven workflow, this study successfully screened a targeted library of five diverse small-molecule ligands. The pipeline evaluated the compounds based on thermodynamic binding affinity, pharmacokinetic viability (ADMET), and structural stability.
## Methodology & Workflow

# Phase 1: Molecular Docking Results
The automated docking phase successfully ranked the five ligands based on their thermodynamic binding affinities (kcal/mol). The pipeline successfully differentiated between high-affinity binders and weak binders:

Top Binders: The three most potent candidates were ligand_5281034 (-9.865 kcal/mol), ligand_5291 (-9.338 kcal/mol), and ligand_123631 (-9.207 kcal/mol), all demonstrating exceptionally strong interactions with the target receptor.
Weak Binders: The pipeline correctly identified ligand_3672 (-6.765 kcal/mol) and ligand_2244 (-5.913 kcal/mol) as having significantly lower binding potential.
Phase 2: ADMET & Pharmacokinetic Profiling
Following docking, the automated ADMET script evaluated the physiological viability of all five compounds.

Drug-Likeness: All five ligands recorded 0 Lipinski Violations, indicating excellent baseline oral bioavailability. Furthermore, the automated data extraction confirmed all candidates possessed High Gastrointestinal (GI) Absorption and were Blood-Brain Barrier (BBB) permeant, with no predicted hepatotoxicity.
The Critical Toxicity Catch: The ADMET filter proved its immense value by identifying a hidden risk in one of the top binders. Despite its excellent docking score (-9.338 kcal/mol), ligand_5291 was flagged for AMES Toxicity (mutagenic potential), disqualifying it as a safe lead compound.
